---
pagination:
  data: games
  size: 1
  alias: refGame
  resolve: values
permalink: "/games/{{ refGame.slug }}/my-reference/"
layout: layouts/game.njk
contentTypeSlug: my-reference
noIndex: true
title: My Reference
---
{#
  The reader's own page, one per game.

  Nothing here is content: the page is a shell, and what fills it is assembled
  in the browser out of the sections that reader has bookmarked. The build
  cannot know what those are, so it renders the empty state and lets the client
  replace it — which is also why the page is kept out of the search index. An
  empty shell indexed once at build time would be the only version anyone ever
  found.
#}
{#
  The game's documents in nav order, each with its sections in reading order.
  This is what lets the page know its own contents before it has fetched a
  single byte: bookmarks say *which* sections, this says what order they go in
  and where they came from, so the table of contents and every heading can be
  rendered immediately and the bodies filled in after.
#}
<script type="application/json" data-reference-order>
  {{ refGame | referenceOrder | dump | safe }}
</script>

<div class="prose" data-my-reference>
  <div class="reference-empty" data-reference-empty>
    <p class="reference-empty__lead">
      Bookmark a section anywhere in {{ refGame.title }} and it is collected
      here, in the order it appears in the rules.
    </p>

    <p class="reference-empty__how">
      Look for
      <span class="reference-empty__demo">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 20" width="13" height="15" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 1.5h11a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v15.2a.6.6 0 0 1-.94.49L8 14.2l-5.56 4a.6.6 0 0 1-.94-.5V2.5a1 1 0 0 1 1-1Z"
                fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/>
        </svg>
      </span>
      beside any section heading — in the rulebook, the summary, an expansion,
      anywhere. Tap it and the whole section is copied onto this page.
    </p>

    <p class="reference-empty__why">
      It is a rules reference you build yourself: the half-dozen things your
      group actually stops to look up, on one page, in one scroll, instead of
      six trips back to the contents. Bookmark as you learn the game, then keep
      this open while you play. It prints, too.
    </p>

    <p class="reference-empty__where">
      Kept in this browser only — nothing is uploaded and nobody else sees it.
      Which also means it stays on this device, goes if you clear your browsing
      data, and is deleted by Safari after about a week away. If you build
      something here worth keeping, Reading preferences will download you a copy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="reference-body" data-reference-body hidden></div>
</div>
